<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  RM_TarGlass.xml           hand-authored, SUMP_TAR_HYDROLOGY_1 ruling 3
  ============================================================================
  NOT emitted by generate_liquid_suite.py and NOT part of RM_Tar.xml
  (Defs/LiquidTypes/TerrainDefs/RM_Tar.xml) — that file is GENERATED and
  edited only via the LIQUID_ROWS table; this is a hand-authored companion
  kept in its own file for exactly that reason (never merge it into the
  generated one).

  This is the PERMANENT terrain Flood_FlowWorks.CoolFrontToGlass writes onto
  the outer rim of a tar release that finishes naturally (FluidDef.
  coolsToGlassEdge on RM_Fluid_Tar, Defs/Canals/FluidDefs/FlowWorks_Fluids.xml)
  — "its edges harden into walkable glass-reach terrain" (ruling 3). FlowWorks
  ships this as its OWN generic def, tier-neutral and donor-free, so glass-
  front cooling works the same for a standalone install as for the campaign
  (biome_mod_architecture.md §7 Q11a's "the free mod looks the same" law,
  generalised from naming to mechanism here). The Utinni layer already ships
  a hand-built, lore-flavoured twin for the player-BUILT case —
  src/RimUtinni/UtinniPatches/Defs/TerrainDefs/RUT_SumpWalkways.xml's own
  RUT_Glasswalk, whose header explicitly deferred "crossing the liquid tar
  itself" to this item — and
  Patches/RUT_Tar_GlasswalkCooling.xml (this same pass) repoints
  coolsToGlassEdge at that terrain instead when UtinniPatches is active, so
  the campaign gets the bespoke art/slip mechanic and everyone else still
  gets a real, working glass front.

  Modelled directly on RUT_Glasswalk's own stats (read in full before writing
  this) rather than invented fresh: no ParentName (a solid floor, not a water
  terrain — IsWater must read false here or Designator_DigCanal would refuse
  to ever dig near it and RM_MapComponent_Excavation.IsSourceCell would
  wrongly treat cooled glass as a natural liquid source), pathCost 3 for the
  same "13/16 ~= 81%, never full speed, ADDS to base cost so this is a
  structural floor not a tuned average" reasoning RUT_SumpWalkways.xml's own
  header derives from GenPath.SpeedPercentString, filthAcceptanceMask None
  (nothing sticks to cooled glass), Flammability 0 (spent/cooled bitumen does
  not reignite — ruling 3 again, and RUT_TarSpent's own "burnt tar is spent"
  precedent). No costList/designationCategory: unlike RUT_Glasswalk this is
  never player-designated, only ever written by code, so it needs no build
  recipe.

  tags carries RM_SlipperyWalkway, the SAME literal tag string
  RUT_SumpWalkways.xml's own header names as the generic hook
  RM_MapComponent_GlasswalkSlip (mandrake.rm.environmentalhazards) already
  watches on ANY terrain — a string tag, not a hard reference, so a cooled
  glass front gets the existing rare-pratfall mechanic for free when that kit
  is active and simply does not when it is absent (no MayRequire needed on a
  tag with no cross-def resolution at all).
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <TerrainDef>
    <defName>RM_TarGlass</defName>
    <label>tar glass</label>
    <description>Tar that spread fast, then cooled fast. Where a flood's own edge outran its heat it hardened into a black glassy sheet -- solid, faintly glossy, and permanent. Walkable, but glass underfoot never lets you run flat out.</description>
    <renderPrecedence>251</renderPrecedence>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterDeepRamp</texturePath>
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <layerable>true</layerable>
    <color>(20,19,22)</color>
    <pathCost>3</pathCost>
    <terrainAffordanceNeeded>Light</terrainAffordanceNeeded>
    <affordances>
      <li>Light</li>
      <li>Medium</li>
      <li>Walkable</li>
    </affordances>
    <filthAcceptanceMask>
      <li>None</li>
    </filthAcceptanceMask>
    <tags>
      <li>RM_SlipperyWalkway</li>
    </tags>
    <statBases>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
      <Beauty>1</Beauty>
    </statBases>
  </TerrainDef>

</Defs>
